    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 210050\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\nIssued by National Weather Service Tucson AZ\n450 PM PST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* A few weak disturbances will roll through the area over the next\n  few days, bringing light snow showers to the Sierra and breezes\n  this afternoon and evening. Otherwise, weather across the rest \n  of the region will remain relatively benign with temperatures \n  trending warmer by next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nWater vapor imagery shows a low pressure center \nover northern Nevada with a minor short wave coming around the \naxis and down along the Sierra Nevada Mountains. Plus, there is a \nslot of dry air shooting across the south Nevada. Hi-res models \nshow this shortwave moving across the region later today and \ntonight, bringing low chances for precipitation for Inyo County \nand isolated areas in southern Nevada. Mountain features will be \nthe main player for any snow or rain shower. \n\nSouthwest portions and any wind prone area will experience \nbreezes this afternoon and late night as the trough swings \nthrough. Then, expect cooler morning temperatures than this \nmorning as stable cold air under a minor ridge moves in tomorrow.\n\nGoing forth into the late weekend and next week. The upper pattern\nwill be flip flopping from ridging to troughing with varying\ndepths and amplitude. The next trough will pass through Sunday to\nMonday, bringing snow chances along the Sierra Crest, general \nbreezes, and mostly cloudy skies for majority of the region. \nEnsembles are decent agreement on ridging over the west for the \nfirst half of the week, leading to a warming trend before \nThanksgiving Day. As for the last half, there is a spread of \nsolutions on the depth of the incoming trough and the amplitude of\nthe ridge over or to the east of Nevada. Therefore, there is\nforecast uncertainty on the evolution, placement, and the \nforecasted impacts of this system. For now, the 8-14 Day CPC \noutlook has a 40-50% lean for above normal temperatures and 33-40%\nlean for above normal precipitation. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...\nWinds remain light around 8 kts becoming variable at times. \nJust after 03z, winds are still on track to favor a more NNW direction\nand likely remain at 5-10 kts. SCT-FEW clouds at times over the \nterminal through the 09z timeframe. Precipitation is not expected \nto impact the terminal, however there is a low (10-20%) chance of \nvicinity showers. If showers occur nearby, could see gusts around \n20 kts. Guidance showing winds staying at a NNW direction through \nthe overnight hours until the early morning hours tomorrow then a \nmore NNE direction around 17z, generally staying light around 6 \nkts. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCaliforniaFor the 00z Forecast Package\nQuiet weather conditions expected to continue through the TAF \nperiod. Few exceptions will be KDAG having strong winds today \naround 15 kts, 25 kt gusts, then becoming around 10 kts, 20 kt \ngusts this afternoon and persist into the evening hours and slowly\ndiminishing overnight. KVGT will have increasing winds this \nafternoon from the north just after 03z through 19z for KVGT \naround 10-12 kts. Guidance is showing precipitation in southern \nNevada but it is not expected to approach the terminal, however \nthere is a low (10-20%) chance of vicinity showers. If showers \noccur nearby, could see gusts around 20 kts. Expect light and \nvariable winds across all other terminals favoring from a \nnortherly direction. There is potential for strong northerly winds\nat KIFP during the early morning hours just after 17z with winds \nup to 10-12 kts. SCT-FEW clouds over most terminals this afternoon\nlasting till around the 8-12z time period and clearing out around\nthe 9-12z time period, with KEED and KIFP expected to have SKC \nconditions through the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Strongman\nAVIATION...Vinson\n\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
